  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
        "intro": "When the denominator has **two** terms, multiplying by one surd does not clear the root — it just moves it. Instead multiply by the **conjugate**, which makes the denominator a difference of two squares with no surd left.",
        "facts": [
          "The **conjugate** of $a+b\\sqrt{c}$ is $a-b\\sqrt{c}$: keep both terms, change only the sign between them.",
          "A conjugate product is a difference of two squares, so it is always rational: $\\left(a+b\\sqrt{c}\\right)\\left(a-b\\sqrt{c}\\right)=a^{2}-b^{2}c$.",
          "$\\dfrac{a}{\\sqrt{b}+\\sqrt{c}}\\times\\dfrac{\\sqrt{b}-\\sqrt{c}}{\\sqrt{b}-\\sqrt{c}}=\\dfrac{a\\sqrt{b}-a\\sqrt{c}}{b-c}$.",
          "Multiplying by $\\dfrac{\\text{conjugate}}{\\text{conjugate}}$ is multiplying by $1$, so the value of the expression does not change.",
          "The denominator can come out negative — for example $\\left(2-\\sqrt{11}\\right)\\left(2+\\sqrt{11}\\right)=4-11=-7$ — so watch the signs when you divide."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "Write down the conjugate of the denominator",
          "Multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate",
          "Expand and simplify"
        ]
      },
